==Norwegian Bokmål==
{{wikipedia|lang=no|T}}

===Etymology 1===
The lowercase form of {{m|nb|T}}. Its modern minuscule shape developed from handwritten forms of Latin {{m|la|T}} and became established through medieval minuscule scripts, particularly Carolingian minuscule, from which modern lowercase Latin letterforms largely descend.

The Latin letter derives through Etruscan {{m|ett|𐌕|tr=t|t=te}} from {{der|nb|grc|Τ|tr=T|t=tau}}, itself borrowed from {{der|nb|phn|𐤕|tr=t|t=taw; literally “mark, sign”}}, ultimately from the Egyptian hieroglyph {{der|nb|egy|𓏴|t=a pair of crossed sticks}}. The Phoenician letter represented {{IPAchar|/t/}}, and Greek and Latin retained both this sound value and its basic crossbar-and-stem form. The Norwegian letter name {{m|nb|te}} reflects the traditional Latin-derived name of the letter.

